         "Category": "Practical",
         "Quiz": "Connecting and testing Bipolar Junction Transistors (BJTs) in DC circuits requires understanding their structure, function, and biasing conditions. BJTs consist of emitter, base, and collector terminals, and correct circuit operation depends on establishing proper voltage and current relationships between these regions. In testing, learners verify transistor orientation (NPN or PNP), junction integrity, and appropriate bias voltages using a digital multimeter or transistor tester.\n\nIn DC circuits, BJTs are often used as amplifiers or switches. Proper connection ensures the base-emitter junction is forward-biased and the collector-base junction is reverse-biased during active operation. Testing confirms that gain (β) and current flow are within expected parameters, which is critical for maintaining signal stability and circuit performance.\n\nBy developing the ability to connect and test BJTs, learners gain essential skills in electronic troubleshooting and system optimization. These procedures support reliable operation of amplifiers, control devices, and other DC electronic systems used throughout industrial and automation applications."
