+        "Summary": [
+          {
+            "Section": "Overview",
+            "Content": "Much electrical work happens overhead. Ladders, scaffolds and lifts get you there — and set up wrong, they're one of the trade's biggest injury sources."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "Key Points",
+            "Content": "Set extension ladders at 4:1 (about 75°) and tie them off. Keep three points of contact. Only fibreglass ladders belong near electrical work. Inspect scaffolds and guardrails before every shift."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "On the Job",
+            "Content": "Running conduit at ceiling height from a baker's scaffold: wheels locked, platform pinned, guardrails up — the setup takes five minutes and removes the biggest risk of the task."
+          }
+        ],
+        "Category": "Practical",
+        "Quiz": "Access equipment: ladder angles and materials, three points of contact, scaffold inspection and safe work at height."
+      }
+    ],
+    "Line D": [
+      {
+        "id": "D1",
+        "Title": "Interpret Plans, Drawings and Specifications",
+        "Summary": [
+          {
+            "Section": "Overview",
+            "Content": "Construction documents describe the finished building: floor plans locate devices, single-line diagrams show the distribution path, schedules list equipment, and specifications define quality and methods."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "Key Points",
+            "Content": "Learn the standard electrical symbols. The single-line diagram is the system's story in one page. Specifications usually govern over drawings when they conflict — check the contract's order of precedence."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "On the Job",
+            "Content": "Laying out a floor's receptacles starts with the architectural plan, the electrical layout and the panel schedule side by side — the three must agree before the first box goes on the wall."
+          }
+        ],
+        "Category": "Theory",
+        "Quiz": "Reading construction documents: floor plans, single-line diagrams, schedules, specifications and the order of precedence between them."
+      },
+      {
+        "id": "D2",
+        "Title": "Describe Trade-Related Documentation",
+        "Summary": [
+          {
+            "Section": "Overview",
+            "Content": "Beyond drawings, a job runs on documents: permits, inspection reports, change orders, as-builts and the apprentice's own logbook recording hours toward certification."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "Key Points",
+            "Content": "Electrical work needs a permit and inspections at defined stages. Change orders capture scope changes in writing. As-built drawings record what was actually installed. Your logbook is the record SkilledTradesBC certifies against."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "On the Job",
+            "Content": "When a wall moves and circuits shift with it, the change order and the marked-up as-built are what keep the next electrician — maybe you, in two years — from hunting for phantom wiring."
+          }
+        ],
+        "Category": "Theory",
+        "Quiz": "Trade documentation: permits and inspections, change orders, as-built drawings and apprenticeship hour logging."
+      },
+      {
+        "id": "D3",
+        "Title": "Plan and Organize Job Tasks",
+        "Summary": [
+          {
+            "Section": "Overview",
+            "Content": "Planning turns drawings into a day's work: what gets installed first, what materials must be on hand, and how your work sequences with other trades."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "Key Points",
+            "Content": "Rough-in follows framing and precedes insulation and drywall — miss the window and the cost multiplies. Stage materials where the work happens. Coordinate with plumbing and HVAC before conflicts are built in."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "On the Job",
+            "Content": "Walking the floor each morning to confirm which areas are framed and which are being boarded tells you where your crew can actually work today — not where the schedule says you should be."
+          }
+        ],
+        "Category": "Practical",
+        "Quiz": "Task planning and sequencing: trade coordination, rough-in windows, material staging and daily planning."
+      },
+      {
+        "id": "D4",
+        "Title": "Prepare a Material Take-Off",
+        "Summary": [
+          {
+            "Section": "Overview",
+            "Content": "A material take-off converts drawings into a counted list of everything the job needs — devices, boxes, conductors, raceway and fittings — so material arrives before the crew does."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "Key Points",
+            "Content": "Count devices from the plans, measure runs for conductor and raceway lengths, and add a sensible waste factor. Group the list by installation phase so ordering matches the schedule."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "On the Job",
+            "Content": "Taking off a suite's wiring: count receptacles, switches and luminaires, measure home runs to the panel, convert to boxes of cable and bundles of conduit — then add 10% because walls are never as straight as drawings."
+          }
+        ],
+        "Category": "Practical",
+        "Quiz": "Material take-offs: counting devices, measuring runs, waste factors and phased ordering."
+      }
+    ],
+    "Line G": [
+      {
+        "id": "G1",
+        "Title": "Describe Effective Workplace Communication",
+        "Summary": [
+          {
+            "Section": "Overview",
+            "Content": "Construction runs on clear, brief and confirmed communication. Instructions, hazards and changes all move by voice, text and drawing markup — and every handoff is a chance for information to distort."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "Key Points",
+            "Content": "Confirm understanding by repeating instructions back. Use trade-standard terms, not slang, for critical items. Write down anything with numbers in it. Bad news travels best fast and factual."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "On the Job",
+            "Content": "A journeyperson calls out 'feed the ceiling boxes from circuit 12' — the apprentice repeats it back before touching a wire. That five-second habit catches misheard numbers before they become miswired circuits."
+          }
+        ],
+        "Category": "Theory",
+        "Quiz": "Workplace communication: confirming instructions, standard terminology, written records and reporting problems early."
+      },
+      {
+        "id": "G2",
+        "Title": "Describe the Mentoring Relationship",
+        "Summary": [
+          {
+            "Section": "Overview",
+            "Content": "Apprenticeship is structured mentoring: a journeyperson demonstrates, the apprentice practises under supervision, and responsibility grows with demonstrated skill."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "Key Points",
+            "Content": "Good mentors explain the why, not just the how. Good apprentices ask questions, accept feedback and show their work. Skills pass in both directions — new workers often carry new techniques and technology."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "On the Job",
+            "Content": "An apprentice who asks 'why bond the box here?' learns code intent, not just habit — and the journeyperson who explains it builds a coworker they can trust with the next panel."
+          }
+        ],
+        "Category": "Theory",
+        "Quiz": "The mentoring relationship: demonstration and practice, feedback, growing responsibility and two-way learning."
+      },
+      {
+        "id": "G3",
+        "Title": "Communicate on the Job Site",
+        "Summary": [
+          {
+            "Section": "Overview",
+            "Content": "Site communication is a practised skill: morning huddles, toolbox talks, hand signals around noise and equipment, and clean markup on drawings that others will read after you."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "Key Points",
+            "Content": "Attend and contribute to toolbox talks. Learn crane and equipment hand signals used on your site. Mark up drawings legibly and date your changes. Radio calls: identify, state, confirm."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "On the Job",
+            "Content": "When the lift operator can't hear over the generator, the standard hand signals are the only language on site — everyone on the crew has to speak them."
+          }
+        ],
+        "Category": "Practical",
+        "Quiz": "Practical site communication: toolbox talks, hand signals, drawing markup and radio discipline."
+      },
+      {
+        "id": "G4",
+        "Title": "Apply Mentoring Techniques",
+        "Summary": [
+          {
+            "Section": "Overview",
+            "Content": "Even a first-year passes skills on — to newer apprentices, helpers and sometimes other trades. Explaining a task clearly is also the best test of whether you truly understand it."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "Key Points",
+            "Content": "Demonstrate slowly, then watch them try. Correct the method, not the person. Break skills into steps small enough to succeed at. Praise in public, correct in private."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "On the Job",
+            "Content": "Showing a new helper how to prep cable ends: demonstrate one, coach one, watch one — then let them run the rest of the box while staying in reach."
+          }
+        ],
+        "Category": "Practical",
+        "Quiz": "Applying mentoring techniques: demonstrate-coach-watch, constructive correction and breaking skills into steps."
+      }
+    ],
+    "Line H": [
+      {
+        "id": "H1",
+        "Title": "Describe Consumer/Supply Service Requirements",
+        "Summary": [
+          {
+            "Section": "Overview",
+            "Content": "The consumer's service is everything that brings utility power to the building's main disconnect: the point of attachment, service conductors, mast or lateral, and the service box itself."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "Key Points",
+            "Content": "Services may be overhead (via a mast) or underground (a lateral). The CEC sets clearances over roofs, driveways and walkways. The service box must open all ungrounded conductors at once and be readily accessible."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "On the Job",
+            "Content": "Planning a 200 A residential service means checking the utility's point-of-attachment height, mast strength, conductor sizing and the clearance over the deck the drawings forgot about."
+          }
+        ],
+        "Category": "Theory",
+        "Quiz": "Consumer services: overhead vs underground supply, clearances, service conductor sizing and service box requirements."
+      },
+      {
+        "id": "H2",
+        "Title": "Describe Metering Equipment",
+        "Summary": [
+          {
+            "Section": "Overview",
+            "Content": "Metering measures the energy a customer uses. Residential services meter directly through a socket-mounted kilowatt-hour meter; larger services meter through instrument transformers."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "Key Points",
+            "Content": "The meter base is utility-sealed — only the utility breaks the seal. Meters record kilowatt-hours; demand meters also record peak draw. Smart meters report remotely and enable time-of-use billing."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "On the Job",
+            "Content": "Wiring a meter base: line side from the service head, load side to the service box, and the bonding intact — then it stays untouched until the utility installs and seals the meter."
+          }
+        ],
+        "Category": "Theory",
+        "Quiz": "Metering equipment: kWh meters, meter bases, utility seals, demand and smart metering."
+      },
+      {
+        "id": "H3",
+        "Title": "Install a Single-Phase Consumer Service",
+        "Summary": [
+          {
+            "Section": "Overview",
+            "Content": "Installing a 120/240 V single-phase service ties together mast, conductors, meter base and service box into the one assembly every other circuit in the building depends on."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "Key Points",
+            "Content": "Size service conductors to the calculated load. Maintain code clearances at the point of attachment. Drip loops keep water out; the mast flashing keeps the roof sealed. Torque service terminations to spec."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "On the Job",
+            "Content": "A service upgrade day is choreographed: utility disconnects, old equipment comes off, new mast and box go in, inspection happens, and the utility reconnects — all before dark."
+          }
+        ],
+        "Category": "Practical",
+        "Quiz": "Installing single-phase services: masts, drip loops, conductor sizing, terminations and inspection sequencing."
+      },
+      {
+        "id": "H4",
+        "Title": "Install Metering Equipment",
+        "Summary": [
+          {
+            "Section": "Overview",
+            "Content": "The meter base sits between the service conductors and the service box, and its installation is shared territory between electrician and utility — built by one, sealed and energized by the other."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "Key Points",
+            "Content": "Mount the base plumb at the utility's specified height. Line and load must land on the correct lugs — reversed metering is a real and expensive mistake. Leave the sealing to the utility."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "On the Job",
+            "Content": "Before calling for the meter, the apprentice re-checks lug torque and line/load orientation, because rework after the utility seals the base means another appointment and a cold house."
+          }
+        ],
+        "Category": "Practical",
+        "Quiz": "Installing meter bases: mounting requirements, line/load orientation, torque and utility coordination."
+      }
+    ],
+    "Line I": [
+      {
+        "id": "I1",
+        "Title": "Describe Overcurrent Protection Devices",
+        "Summary": [
+          {
+            "Section": "Overview",
+            "Content": "Overcurrent devices — fuses and circuit breakers — open a circuit when current exceeds safe limits, protecting conductors and equipment from overload and short-circuit damage."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "Key Points",
+            "Content": "Overload is too much current for too long; short circuit is a massive instantaneous fault. Devices carry a current rating, a voltage rating and an interrupting rating that must meet or exceed available fault current."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "On the Job",
+            "Content": "A 15 A breaker on 14 AWG copper is matched protection — swap in a 20 A breaker and the wire becomes the fuse. Ratings exist as a set, never individually."
+          }
+        ],
+        "Category": "Theory",
+        "Quiz": "Overcurrent protection: overload vs short circuit, fuses and breakers, current/voltage/interrupting ratings."
+      },
+      {
+        "id": "I2",
+        "Title": "Describe GFCI and AFCI Protection",
+        "Summary": [
+          {
+            "Section": "Overview",
+            "Content": "GFCIs protect people: they compare current out and back, and trip in milliseconds when even 5 mA leaks through an unintended path — like a person. AFCIs protect property by recognizing the signature of dangerous arcing."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "Key Points",
+            "Content": "GFCI: 5 mA imbalance, Class A, required near water. AFCI: detects series and parallel arcs, required in dwelling living areas. Both come as breakers or receptacles, and both have test buttons that should actually be used."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "On the Job",
+            "Content": "A hair dryer dropped in a sink doesn't blow the breaker — the GFCI's 5 mA trip is what stands between that moment and electrocution. Code placement rules follow the water."
+          }
+        ],
+        "Category": "Theory",
+        "Quiz": "GFCI and AFCI protection: trip principles, thresholds, code locations and testing."
+      },
+      {
+        "id": "I3",
+        "Title": "Install Circuit Breakers and Fuses",
+        "Summary": [
+          {
+            "Section": "Overview",
+            "Content": "Installing protection is precision work: the device rating must match conductor ampacity, the panel listing must accept the breaker model, and terminations must be torqued to spec."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "Key Points",
+            "Content": "Only install breakers listed for the panel. Match rating to the smallest conductor protected. Torque matters — loose terminations heat and fail. Fuse holders: line side to the fixed contact so removal is dead."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "On the Job",
+            "Content": "Populating a new panel: schedule in hand, breakers matched to each circuit's conductor size, torque screwdriver on every lug, and labels written before the cover goes on."
+          }
+        ],
+        "Category": "Practical",
+        "Quiz": "Installing breakers and fuses: panel listing, rating matching, torque and labelling."
+      },
+      {
+        "id": "I4",
+        "Title": "Install GFCI and AFCI Devices",
+        "Summary": [
+          {
+            "Section": "Overview",
+            "Content": "GFCI and AFCI devices install like their ordinary cousins but add line/load discipline: wired wrong, they either don't protect or trip constantly."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "Key Points",
+            "Content": "Line and load terminals are not interchangeable. Downstream protection flows through the load terminals. Shared neutrals break AFCI/GFCI breakers — each needs its own neutral back to the device. Test after installing."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "On the Job",
+            "Content": "A bathroom string: one GFCI receptacle at the first outlet protects everything downstream through its load terminals — and the sticker kit tells the next person which outlets those are."
+          }
+        ],
+        "Category": "Practical",
+        "Quiz": "Installing GFCI/AFCI: line vs load, downstream protection, neutral discipline and functional testing."
+      }
+    ],
+    "Line J": [
+      {
+        "id": "J1",
+        "Title": "Describe Low Voltage Distribution Equipment",
+        "Summary": [
+          {
+            "Section": "Overview",
+            "Content": "Distribution equipment moves power from the service to the loads: panelboards split it into branch circuits, splitters divide large feeds, and disconnects give each portion a working isolation point."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "Key Points",
+            "Content": "Panelboards hold branch and feeder breakers on energized busbars. Splitters house tap connections for multiple disconnects. Everything carries voltage, current and fault ratings that must match the system."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "On the Job",
+            "Content": "Tracing a building's power: service box → splitter → three disconnects → three panels. Drawing that path from the equipment in front of you is the first step of every troubleshooting call."
+          }
+        ],
+        "Category": "Theory",
+        "Quiz": "Distribution equipment: panelboards, splitters, disconnects and their ratings."
+      },
+      {
+        "id": "J2",
+        "Title": "Interpret Panelboard Schedules",
+        "Summary": [
+          {
+            "Section": "Overview",
+            "Content": "The panel schedule is the map of a panelboard: every breaker's rating, its circuit number and what it feeds. An accurate schedule is the difference between isolating one circuit and guessing at six."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "Key Points",
+            "Content": "Odd circuit numbers stack on one side, even on the other, alternating busbars top to bottom. Balanced loading across phases reduces neutral current. Blank means spare — verify before trusting."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "On the Job",
+            "Content": "Before pulling a permit for added circuits, the schedule shows whether the panel has physical space and spare capacity — or whether the quote just grew a sub-panel."
+          }
+        ],
+        "Category": "Theory",
+        "Quiz": "Panel schedules: circuit numbering, busbar alternation, load balancing and capacity checks."
+      },
+      {
+        "id": "J3",
+        "Title": "Install Panelboards and Splitters",
+        "Summary": [
+          {
+            "Section": "Overview",
+            "Content": "Mounting distribution equipment sets up everything that follows: level, accessible, code-clear of obstructions, and connected with properly sized and torqued conductors."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "Key Points",
+            "Content": "Maintain working space clearances in front of equipment. Height limits put the top breaker within reach. Knockouts get connectors, unused openings get plugs. Torque bus and lug connections to the label."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "On the Job",
+            "Content": "Setting a 42-circuit panel: plumb and level so covers fit, working space verified before mounting, feeder landed and torqued, and the torque values written on the inspection card."
+          }
+        ],
+        "Category": "Practical",
+        "Quiz": "Installing panelboards and splitters: working space, mounting, terminations and torque."
+      },
+      {
+        "id": "J4",
+        "Title": "Connect Feeders and Sub-Panels",
+        "Summary": [
+          {
+            "Section": "Overview",
+            "Content": "Sub-panels extend distribution to garages, suites and outbuildings. The feeder that supplies them must be sized, protected and — critically — grounded correctly."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "Key Points",
+            "Content": "In a sub-panel, neutrals and grounds separate: the neutral floats on an isolated bar, bonding lands on the enclosure. The feeder's overcurrent device lives at its source end. Four wires to a detached building, per current code."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "On the Job",
+            "Content": "Wiring a garage sub-panel: four-conductor feeder, neutral bar isolation kit installed, bonding screw removed — the three details inspectors check first, because they're the three most often wrong."
+          }
+        ],
+        "Category": "Practical",
+        "Quiz": "Feeders and sub-panels: neutral/ground separation, feeder protection and detached building requirements."
+      }
+    ],
+    "Line L": [
+      {
+        "id": "L1",
+        "Title": "Describe Grounding and Bonding Principles",
+        "Summary": [
+          {
+            "Section": "Overview",
+            "Content": "Grounding connects the electrical system to earth, stabilizing its voltage; bonding connects all exposed metal parts together so a fault can't make one of them dangerous to touch."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "Key Points",
+            "Content": "Grounding is for the system; bonding is for people. A fault on a bonded system has a low-impedance path that trips the overcurrent device fast. Earth itself is a poor conductor — bonding, not dirt, clears faults."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "On the Job",
+            "Content": "Why does the metal box get a bond wire? So that if a hot conductor touches it, the breaker trips instantly instead of the box waiting silently at 120 V for someone's hand."
+          }
+        ],
+        "Category": "Theory",
+        "Quiz": "Grounding vs bonding: system grounding, fault current paths and why bonding protects people."
+      },
+      {
+        "id": "L2",
+        "Title": "Describe Ground Fault Detection",
+        "Summary": [
+          {
+            "Section": "Overview",
+            "Content": "On systems that operate ungrounded — some industrial plants and marinas — ground fault detection signals the first fault so it can be found and fixed before a second fault causes a short circuit or outage."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "Key Points",
+            "Content": "The first ground fault on an ungrounded system doesn't trip anything — it just re-references the system. Detection lamps or relays announce it. The second fault, without repair, is a phase-to-phase short."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "On the Job",
+            "Content": "Three detection lamps glowing evenly means a healthy system; one dark lamp points at the faulted phase. Reading them is the plant electrician's morning glance."
+          }
+        ],
+        "Category": "Theory",
+        "Quiz": "Ground fault detection on ungrounded systems: first-fault behaviour, indicator lamps and relays."
+      },
+      {
+        "id": "L3",
+        "Title": "Install System Grounding",
+        "Summary": [
+          {
+            "Section": "Overview",
+            "Content": "System grounding starts at the grounding electrode: rods, plates or a concrete-encased electrode connected by an unbroken grounding conductor to the service equipment."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "Key Points",
+            "Content": "Ground rods drive full depth; pairs space at least 3 m apart. The grounding conductor runs unspliced and protected. Connections use listed clamps — accessible ones stay accessible."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "On the Job",
+            "Content": "Two rods, ten feet of buried #6 to the service, clamps rated for direct burial: twenty minutes of work that every circuit in the house silently depends on."
+          }
+        ],
+        "Category": "Practical",
+        "Quiz": "Installing grounding electrodes: rods and spacing, grounding conductor rules and listed connections."
+      },
+      {
+        "id": "L4",
+        "Title": "Install Bonding Conductors",
+        "Summary": [
+          {
+            "Section": "Overview",
+            "Content": "Bonding ties together boxes, raceways, water pipes, gas piping and equipment enclosures so everything a person can touch stays at the same potential during a fault."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "Key Points",
+            "Content": "Size bonding conductors from the code tables based on the overcurrent device. Metal raceway can serve as the bond if its joints are made up tight. Water meters get jumpers; isolated sections get connected."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "On the Job",
+            "Content": "Bonding the water service: clamp upstream of the meter, jumper across it, conductor sized to the table — because a corroded meter gasket shouldn't decide whether a fault clears."
+          }
+        ],
+        "Category": "Practical",
+        "Quiz": "Installing bonding: conductor sizing, raceway continuity, water and gas piping bonds."
+      }
+    ],
+    "Line Q": [
+      {
+        "id": "Q1",
+        "Title": "Describe Raceway and Cable Types",
+        "Summary": [
+          {
+            "Section": "Overview",
+            "Content": "Conductors travel in raceways (EMT, rigid, PVC, flexible conduits) or as cables (NMD90, armoured AC90/TECK, underground types) — each with an environment it's designed for and rules that follow."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "Key Points",
+            "Content": "EMT for dry interiors; rigid where damage threatens; PVC underground and in corrosive areas; NMD90 in dry residential walls; TECK where things get industrial. Fill tables limit how many conductors share a raceway."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "On the Job",
+            "Content": "Choosing between EMT and TECK for a parkade run comes down to environment: moisture, mechanical damage and what the spec already decided. The catalogue is big; the drawings narrow it."
+          }
+        ],
+        "Category": "Theory",
+        "Quiz": "Raceway and cable types: EMT, rigid, PVC, NMD90, AC90, TECK and where each belongs."
+      },
+      {
+        "id": "Q2",
+        "Title": "Bend and Install Conduit",
+        "Summary": [
+          {
+            "Section": "Overview",
+            "Content": "Conduit bending is the trade's signature craft: 90s, offsets, saddles and kicks measured and bent so runs land square, parallel and within the 360° bend limit between pulls."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "Key Points",
+            "Content": "Learn your bender's take-up and deduct. Offsets use paired bends and multiplier tables. Total bends between pull points stay at or under 360°. Support spacing and strap requirements come from code tables."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "On the Job",
+            "Content": "A clean rack of parallel offsets around a beam is the difference between a journeyperson's run and a fight with the wire during every future pull."
+          }
+        ],
+        "Category": "Practical",
+        "Quiz": "Conduit bending: 90s, offsets, saddles, the 360-degree rule and support spacing."
+      },
+      {
+        "id": "Q3",
+        "Title": "Pull and Terminate Cables",
+        "Summary": [
+          {
+            "Section": "Overview",
+            "Content": "Getting conductors through raceway without damage takes planning: swab the run, use pulling compound, respect maximum pulling tension and leave enough tail for terminations."
+          },
+          {
+            "Section": "Key Points",
+            "Content": "Pull together whatever shares the raceway — never add to an occupied conduit. Lubricate long or bendy pulls. Support cable trays and secure cables at code intervals. Label both ends before walking away."
